A||||6||billfredmike24||clutch cost?||||5
Z||000000||billfredmike24||11-18-1999||03:06 PM||||has anyone in ontario had a clutch replaced on an 85 gt or a v6 engine.  How much did it cost and where did you get it done.  I had price given to me at around $1000.||||reg||5
Z||000001||kornholio||11-18-1999||05:47 PM||||That $1000 clutch replacement cost is what made me buy an auto.  I really wanted a standard!  $1000 seems to be the average price in Ontario.  However, if you are lucky enough to find a "back-yard" mechanic, they would probably do the job for about $500.||||reg||9
Z||000002||four50four||11-18-1999||06:26 PM||||you could do it yourslef and save a LOT of money...||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-18-1999||06:26 PM||||Well I'm getting ready to do my clutch myself and I'll say I'm saving a lot of money in labor.  But, them money I'm saving in labor I'm spending on other parts to replace while the engine is out.  I bought a RAM heavy duty clutch $230, master cylinder $60, slave cylinder $70, timing chain set $50, all belts $10, intake manifold gasket $?? (forgot how much), jackstands $40, flywheel (i hope not), and some random other things. Total cost is going to be around $500 and some scraped knuckles. Not too shabby, yet. Once I do the job I may have another opinion. <BR>All prices are approximate, I don't have the receipts right next to me. All parts I've gotten from: The Fiero Store 4 days and $10 shipping, Wrenchead.com 2 days and free shipping, and Pepboys just down the street. With Wrenchead.com being my favorite.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-18-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||Scott2M4||11-19-1999||07:36 AM||||Let us know the outcome, fiero56.  This will be good info for the archive!||||reg||14
Z||000005||fiero56||11-19-1999||09:52 AM||||I'm going to try to take some pictures and write up an article on how to do everything.  Ed Parks has been a great help as I prepared for this. The procedure is supposed to be "slightly" different for the '88s than the '84-'87.  Something about not being able to drop the entire suspension with the engine. <BR>I think I have everything I need. Clutch came in last night, looks good.  This is my first clutch replacement/engine removal so we'll see how it goes(all I have is my dorky haynes manual). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000006||2birds||11-21-1999||01:31 AM||||I did the clutch on my 86 5-speed GT myself 3 years ago.  I was quoted 8.1 hours at $60/hr, plus parts.  I got a RAM HD kit from summit for around $180.  The biggest thing was I had access to a lift and a transmission jack.  I opted to build an engine holder like the haynes manual shows, and leave the engine in place.  Hindsight makes me think it is probably easier to drop the whole subframe and drivetrain as a unit and unbolt and remove the engine from the cradle with a cherry-picker type hoist.  The hassle of trying to align the input shaft in the disk splines with the limited apace available, and overhead at that, far exceeds the pains of disconnecting the engine.  I also replaced my rear main seal, oil pan gasket, and put in the poly subframe bushings (extra cost if I paid someone else).  Extra parts I had to buy were a donut for the head pipe flange, new bolts for the head pipe flange (more fun), flywheel and pressure plate bolts(cheap insurance), a tamper proof torx bit for the trunk lid hinge, and get the flywheel resurfaced. I was able to do most of this by myself,, except for wrestling the transaxle in place, and it took two eight hour days, since I had not done a clutch since my VW in '84.  BTW, the local Pontiac dealer wouldn't touch it unless I bought the parts from them ($$$$$).  Good luck.  ||||reg||1
